From: USA-RedDragon <USA-RedDragon@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 03:03:50 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] fix(turns): a restart starts the turn of a re-placed queued
 message, and ACP runs a handed-back steer

Patch 0098 kept a queued message across a failed turn and a restart, and handed a steer back as result["pending_steer"]. Two of its paths still waited for the user. After a restart the never-drained queue row is re-placed at the transcript end as an open user turn, and nothing started it: it was read with the next user message or an auto-continue, though the hub had told the user it would run. And the ACP surface ignored pending_steer, so a steer handed back from a failed turn was in no queue (the hub and the messaging gateway run it as the next turn).

The re-placed copy now carries display_metadata._requeued_prompt. The mount hook that already recovers a crash-interrupted turn (_maybe_schedule_auto_continue, with no crash marker) starts the turn of a re-placed prompt once the chat is mounted idle. It takes the same admissions as the auto-continue turn: no running turn, no Stop, no other live owner of the session lease. The claim (SessionDB.claim_requeued_prompts) clears the marker in one transaction and only looks at the trailing run of marked user rows, so a second mount, a sibling backend or a later restart finds nothing, and a message that already has a reply after it is never rerun. Stop now deactivates the accept rows of the prompts it cancels (SessionDB.drop_cancelled_queue_rows), so a cancelled message is never re-placed or started. The turn replaces the hydrated copy of the message in history, so the model sees it once.

ACP _finish_turn appends result["pending_steer"] to the session queue before it drains, so the steer runs as the next turn through the same drain the queued prompts use.
